#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a47b88ad-e6a3-548e-b3a7-95ede04a074e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288553} {:answer/id #uuid "28e902a6-5051-5cca-b30e-0fee2f148317" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288554} {:answer/id #uuid "b5b15967-d3ef-551a-947f-bfb28904e4a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288555} {:answer/id #uuid "debcc437-3577-57c9-b607-c2c9c289e770" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288556} {:answer/id #uuid "4e80adb5-d2d0-5375-ac8e-96c908f4c86d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e80adb5-d2d0-5375-ac8e-96c908f4c86d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288557} :answer-field/id #uuid "3906e258-840c-5de5-af0e-44d141899def" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "760ba6ba-f519-5e68-b2c7-ade22689c6e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-18284" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{22}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A$ $=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ if its determinant is $-16.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{22}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{22}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{22} & =(-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{22}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{22}=\\frac{1}{-16}\\cdot 4=-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee3e2b62-ecd3-52a1-9ef1-f87f4614c55c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288560} {:answer/id #uuid "43a3fbd2-7f9b-5efe-b807-f44b9545e81a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43a3fbd2-7f9b-5efe-b807-f44b9545e81a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288561} :answer-field/id #uuid "197b5c36-1e0b-5239-8e5a-8e1d24c83ee2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7fedf1ce-1d8a-575c-bbd4-9093e6ecd764" :question/math-academy-id "q-275755" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n${a}_{32}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}| & =3|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}|-0|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|+(-3)|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(2+2)-0-3(2-0) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{32}^{t}={c}_{23}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{23} & =(-1)^{2+3}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(6-0) \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{32}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{32}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{23} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55b1616d-08df-5ad7-ba02-103428efcd35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55b1616d-08df-5ad7-ba02-103428efcd35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288564} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fb06bcc-74a6-5677-a4f1-bd2028bab163" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288563}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bfe6bc14-6591-5f71-bb33-e7e4cf7f033e" :question/math-academy-id "q-319413" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n${a}_{12}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| & =2|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|-0|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|+(-1)|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(6-0)-0(-2-0)-1(1-0) \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}^{t}={c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(0+1) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288565 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d79fb5d1-22a6-511d-90ca-7d66c540d172" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cofactor Method To Solve for an Unknown Quantity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cfddfff-2efc-5935-a57b-be628caa2616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288567} {:answer/id #uuid "69b0dac9-0505-5959-b6b1-5693c223ec42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288568} {:answer/id #uuid "88892a86-3a54-5a28-8119-ff95f09f7e71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288569} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd800ed-e50c-554d-9737-eeda0629a09b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288570} {:answer/id #uuid "4ed7a78d-bd89-5f9b-94b1-6f24d665fcd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88892a86-3a54-5a28-8119-ff95f09f7e71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288569} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8b32d26-4ce1-5e64-80c3-4102cfcaad63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f2b1695b-282d-54b6-9ac9-0c3ba20a588b" :question/math-academy-id "q-73542" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ k & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $1.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ k & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| & =1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|-(-1)|\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2-0)+(-2k-0)+0 \\\\  & =-2k-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{23}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{23} & =(-1)^{2+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(1-(-3)) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{32}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{23} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2k-2}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{k+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{32}^{′}=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{k+1} & =1 \\\\ 2 & =k+1 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d79fb5d1-22a6-511d-90ca-7d66c540d172" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cofactor Method To Solve for an Unknown Quantity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b92df9bf-57f8-52c4-bdb1-778f42842a6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288574} {:answer/id #uuid "30d4d4f3-edf3-50e0-b558-676c2c519051" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288575} {:answer/id #uuid "88d0c1ad-2e69-53e8-b0f1-14231d6b6c48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288576} {:answer/id #uuid "104c5573-009e-55dc-872f-3406140f4ce6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288577} {:answer/id #uuid "42c0d506-1aa1-50f1-925f-3776c434998d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30d4d4f3-edf3-50e0-b558-676c2c519051" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288575} :answer-field/id #uuid "32185872-743f-5625-abaf-14cc3904b386" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b53bfca7-a35b-5bdd-8a26-779771b1f95b" :question/math-academy-id "q-9980" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & k & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $\\frac{2}{5}.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & k & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =2|\\begin{aligned}k & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-3|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}4 & k \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(k-2)-3(4-6)+1(4-3k) \\\\  & =2k-4+6+4-3k \\\\  & =6-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{12} & =(-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(4-6) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{21}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6-k}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{6-k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{21}^{′}=\\frac{2}{5}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{6-k} & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 2\\cdot 5 & =2(6-k) \\\\ 10 & =12-2k \\\\ 2k & =2 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
